{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful ruler and a shrewd diplomat navigating complex political landscapes. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and extensive efforts to consolidate his power and protect Tripoli from its enemies primarily the Seljuk Turks and later the Ayyubids.\n\n*Early Life and Accession* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were challenging as he faced threats from various factions within the county and external pressures from neighboring powers. He quickly established his authority through military strength and political maneuvering securing his position as ruler. He focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and fostering economic prosperity through trade.\n\nRaymond III engaged extensively in Crusading activities participating in several major campaigns. He fought alongside King Baldwin of Jerusalem during the Second Crusade and later played a crucial role in the Third Crusade. His leadership and military prowess were highly regarded. He was a key figure in negotiating alliances and securing support for the Crusader states. He demonstrated strategic brilliance in battle often employing defensive tactics to overcome numerically superior forces.\n\n*Diplomacy and Political Alliances* A significant aspect of Raymond III's reign was his skillful diplomacy. He understood the importance of alliances in maintaining Tripoli's independence. He forged relationships with various rulers including the Byzantine Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa and the Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI. He also engaged in complex negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to maintain a fragile peace. These diplomatic efforts were essential for protecting Tripoli's interests and ensuring its survival in a volatile region. He understood that military strength alone was insufficient and prioritized building a network of allies.\n\nRaymond III faced increasing challenges from the Ayyubids under Saladin. He attempted to maintain a cautious relationship with Saladin seeking to avoid open conflict while preserving Tripoli's autonomy. He strategically used diplomacy and military preparedness to deter Ayyubid aggression. He also invested in strengthening Tripoli's fortifications and military capabilities preparing for potential future conflicts.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. He is remembered as one of the most successful and influential Counts of Tripoli. His reign was characterized by stability prosperity and military strength. He successfully defended Tripoli against numerous threats and maintained its independence for several decades. He left behind a well-defended county a thriving economy and a reputation as a capable and resourceful ruler. His legacy continues to be studied by historians interested in the Crusades and the history of the Levant. His reign represents a high point in the history of the County of Tripoli a testament to his leadership and diplomatic skills.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.00023135847004596144}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5987286790041253e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.5